<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cusco School</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Cuzco School</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a> School was a tradition of Catholic religious painting centred on Cusco in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-peru">Viceroyalty of Peru</a> from the late sixteenth to the eighteenth century. Its largely indigenous and mestizo painters adapted European models, adding Andean plants, animals and dress, flattened perspective and lavish gold brocade. Its works, such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corpus-christi-feast">Corpus Christi</a> series of Cusco, were produced in large numbers for churches across the Andes.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cusco-school.jpg" alt="Cusco School" width="320" height="432" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Virgin_of_Carmel_Saving_Souls_in_Purgatory_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
